The Brightsen Nucleon Cluster Model decomposes the wave function of a composite nucleus as a superposition of partial wave functions corresponding to all admissible ways of partitioning its nucleons into close-packed clusters built from three fundamental two- and three-nucleon constituents {NP, NPN, PNP} (together with halo clusters such as NN, PP, NNN), and allows clusters of matter to be paired with clusters of antimatter to form so-called "unmatter" entities inside nuclei. Based on this formalism, the model issues the following experimentally testable predictions:

(a) a specific formula for the average number of prompt neutrons emitted per spontaneous or induced fission event;
(b) the occurrence of low-energy nuclear transformations (low-temperature/low-energy nuclear reactions) explained as cluster rearrangements;
(c) the existence of unmatter entities (bound combinations of matter and antimatter clusters) inside ordinary nuclei;
(d) antigravity signatures associated with the antimatter components of such unmatter clusters;
(e) the possibility of induced transmutation of radioactive nuclei via cluster rearrangement;
(f) a sub-cluster structure for the ⁴He nucleus;
(g) new effective intranuclear forces arising from cluster–cluster interactions, not present in standard single-nucleon descriptions;
(h) the existence of a new closed-shell ("magic") nucleus at N = 172, Z = 106, A = 278 (a superheavy seaborgium isotope);
(i) a cluster-based mechanism accounting quantitatively for the anomalously large thermal-neutron absorption cross section of ¹⁰B.

Problem. Determine which (if any) of the predictions (a)–(i) are correct: for each prediction, establish either a rigorous derivation from the cluster-model formalism together with experimental confirmation, or a rigorous refutation, either mathematical or experimental.

